neutron-zzzzzzzzzh-wic-skip-seed-for-pristine-bitmaps

d2d1/windowscodecs: don't seed a WIC render target from a bitmap that has never held a pixel.

BACKGROUND. neutron-zzzzzzzzzd made a D2D render target over a WIC bitmap start out holding
the bitmap's pixels, which is what a RETAINED target must do and what fixed Premiere's missing
time ruler. It pays for that with a full-surface CPU->GPU upload (Lock + UpdateSubresource) on
every target creation and every pool reuse.

MEASURED COST (Premiere, NEUTRON_D2D_PERF=1, ~60s of timeline scrolling):
    d2d_factory_CreateWicBitmapRenderTarget   177us/call (July baseline, pre-seed)
                                              334us/call (with the seed)
                                              17,158 calls in the capture

MEASURED WASTE (WINEDEBUG=+nwicgpu, same capture):
    attached=15946  served=15798  materialized=274
Only 274 of 17,158 bitmaps had ANY content. The rest are freshly calloc'd -- transparent
black -- so the upload wrote a megabyte of zeroes into an already-zero texture, ~280x a
second, for no observable effect.

FIX. windowscodecs tracks whether a bitmap has ever been written: FALSE at creation only when
it wraps caller-supplied memory, and cleared permanently by a write lock or a GPU source
attach. d2d1 asks before seeding; a pristine bitmap gets the plain clear instead, which is
free and GPU-side.

⚠️ THE CLEAR IS LOAD-BEARING, do not "simplify" this to an early return. A pooled target that
skips both the seed and the clear publishes the PREVIOUS TENANT's pixels -- that is the white
striped bar across the timeline recorded in PERFORMANCE.md. The seed function therefore
reports whether it ran, and the caller clears whenever it did not.

⚠️ The query is a SEPARATE IID (INeutronWicGpuBitmap2), not a method appended to the v1 vtbl.
d2d1 and windowscodecs are separate DLLs and this project stages individual DLLs into runtimes
routinely, so a mismatched pair is a realistic assembly; a grown vtbl would be a call into
whatever follows it. A missing v2 fails QueryInterface and the caller seeds unconditionally --
the old, correct, slower behaviour.

RESULT: user-confirmed correct (ruler, grey in/out highlight, no white strip). Cost moved
334 -> 298 us/call and forced materializations 274 -> 115, but BOTH runs differ in workload
(17.2k vs 19.6k calls) and this project's own rule is that cross-run d2d1 comparison is noise
-- EndDraw and BeginDraw moved UP between the same two runs. So: correct and directionally
right, magnitude NOT established. Establishing it needs per-frame alternation inside one
session, the method that validated lazy tessellation.

Next target from the same capture, and NOT a regression of ours: d2d_device_context_EndDraw,
now the top cost at ~343us x 19.6k calls -- the GPU->CPU sync the July notes flagged as
"explicitly requested, never investigated".

--- a/dlls/d2d1/wic_render_target.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/wic_render_target.c
@@ -436,7 +436,10 @@
  *
  * Seeding the texture from the bitmap makes the target retained the way the API
  * says it is. Failure is non-fatal: it leaves the old blank-start behaviour. */
-static void d2d_wic_render_target_seed_from_bitmap(struct d2d_wic_render_target *rt)
+/* Returns TRUE when the texture now holds the bitmap's pixels. FALSE means the caller must
+ * fall back to clearing: either there was nothing to seed from, or the bitmap is pristine
+ * (transparent) and a clear is both correct and free. */
+static BOOL d2d_wic_render_target_seed_from_bitmap(struct d2d_wic_render_target *rt)
 {
     IWICBitmapLock *lock;
     ID3D10Resource *resource;
@@ -446,14 +449,36 @@
     BYTE *data;
 
     if (!rt->bitmap || !rt->bpp)
-        return;
+        return FALSE;
+
+    /* NEUTRON PERF: a bitmap that has never held a pixel has nothing to retain, and seeding
+     * it costs a full-surface CPU->GPU upload for no effect. MEASURED in Premiere: 17,158
+     * seeds during a minute of timeline scrolling, of which only 274 bitmaps had any content
+     * -- and CreateWicBitmapRenderTarget went 177us -> 334us per call when this seed was
+     * added. Ask, and let the caller fall back to the plain clear.
+     *
+     * A bitmap that cannot answer (native windowscodecs, an older DLL in a mixed runtime) is
+     * treated as having content, which is the slow but always-correct answer. */
+    {
+        INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *gpu2;
+        BOOL pristine = FALSE;
+
+        if (SUCCEEDED(IWICBitmap_QueryInterface(rt->bitmap, &IID_INeutronWicGpuBitmap2,
+                (void **)&gpu2)))
+        {
+            HRESULT hr = gpu2->lpVtbl->IsPristine(gpu2, &pristine);
+            gpu2->lpVtbl->Release(gpu2);
+            if (SUCCEEDED(hr) && pristine)
+                return FALSE;
+        }
+    }
 
     rect.X = 0;
     rect.Y = 0;
     rect.Width = rt->width;
     rect.Height = rt->height;
     if (FAILED(IWICBitmap_Lock(rt->bitmap, &rect, WICBitmapLockRead, &lock)))
-        return;
+        return FALSE;
 
     if (SUCCEEDED(IWICBitmapLock_GetDataPointer(lock, &size, &data))
             && SUCCEEDED(IWICBitmapLock_GetStride(lock, &pitch)))
@@ -469,6 +494,7 @@
     }
 
     IWICBitmapLock_Release(lock);
+    return TRUE;
 }
 
 /* NEUTRON WIC PAINT TEST (NEUTRON_D2D_WICPAINT=1): overwrite every wide-and-short
@@ -770,9 +796,9 @@
      * d2d_wic_render_target_seed_from_bitmap). The clear stays as the fallback for
      * when there is no bitmap to seed from, since publishing the previous tenant's
      * pixels would be worse than publishing nothing. */
-    if (rt->bitmap)
+    if (d2d_wic_render_target_seed_from_bitmap(rt))
     {
-        d2d_wic_render_target_seed_from_bitmap(rt);
+        /* texture now matches the bitmap; nothing further to do */
     }
     else if (rt->pool_rtv)
     {
--- a/dlls/windowscodecs/bitmap.c
+++ b/dlls/windowscodecs/bitmap.c
@@ -67,6 +67,11 @@
     const struct neutron_wicgpu_source *gpu_ops;    /* NULL => ordinary CPU bitmap */
     void *gpu_ctx;
     BOOL gpu_was_read;      /* diagnostic only: did anyone actually read this frame? */
+    /* NEUTRON: TRUE until a pixel is written by ANY route. A D2D render target created over
+     * a pristine bitmap has nothing to retain, so it can skip the full-surface seed upload
+     * (see INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 in wine/neutron_wicgpu.h). */
+    BOOL pristine;
+    INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 INeutronWicGpuBitmap2_iface;
 } BitmapImpl;
 
 typedef struct BitmapLockImpl {
@@ -333,6 +338,10 @@
          * bitmap implemented elsewhere -- simply keeps doing the copy. */
         *ppv = &This->INeutronWicGpuBitmap_iface;
     }
+    else if (IsEqualIID(&IID_INeutronWicGpuBitmap2, iid))
+    {
+        *ppv = &This->INeutronWicGpuBitmap2_iface;
+    }
     else
     {
         if (IsEqualIID(&IID_CMetaBitmapRenderTarget, iid))
@@ -530,6 +539,10 @@
         LeaveCriticalSection(&This->cs);
     }
 
+    /* NEUTRON: a write lock hands out a raw pointer, so assume it is used. */
+    if (flags & WICBitmapLockWrite)
+        This->pristine = FALSE;
+
     result = malloc(sizeof(BitmapLockImpl));
     if (!result)
         return E_OUTOFMEMORY;
@@ -635,6 +648,10 @@
     bitmap_gpu_drop(This, TRUE);
     This->gpu_ops = ops;
     This->gpu_ctx = ctx;
+    /* NEUTRON: an attached source is content, and stays content after it materialises into
+     * This->data -- so this is a permanent transition out of pristine, not a state that
+     * follows gpu_ops. */
+    if (ops) This->pristine = FALSE;
     LeaveCriticalSection(&This->cs);
 
     if (ops)
@@ -653,6 +670,51 @@
     neutron_wicgpu_SetSource,
 };
 
+static inline BitmapImpl *impl_from_INeutronWicGpuBitmap2(INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *iface)
+{
+    return CONTAINING_RECORD(iface, BitmapImpl, INeutronWicGpuBitmap2_iface);
+}
+
+static HRESULT STDMETHODCALLTYPE neutron_wicgpu2_QueryInterface(INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *iface,
+        REFIID iid, void **out)
+{
+    BitmapImpl *This = impl_from_INeutronWicGpuBitmap2(iface);
+    return IWICBitmap_QueryInterface(&This->IWICBitmap_iface, iid, out);
+}
+
+static ULONG STDMETHODCALLTYPE neutron_wicgpu2_AddRef(INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *iface)
+{
+    BitmapImpl *This = impl_from_INeutronWicGpuBitmap2(iface);
+    return IWICBitmap_AddRef(&This->IWICBitmap_iface);
+}
+
+static ULONG STDMETHODCALLTYPE neutron_wicgpu2_Release(INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *iface)
+{
+    BitmapImpl *This = impl_from_INeutronWicGpuBitmap2(iface);
+    return IWICBitmap_Release(&This->IWICBitmap_iface);
+}
+
+static HRESULT STDMETHODCALLTYPE neutron_wicgpu2_IsPristine(INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *iface,
+        BOOL *pristine)
+{
+    BitmapImpl *This = impl_from_INeutronWicGpuBitmap2(iface);
+
+    if (!pristine) return E_POINTER;
+    EnterCriticalSection(&This->cs);
+    /* An attached GPU source IS content, even though no CPU write has happened. */
+    *pristine = This->pristine && !This->gpu_ops;
+    LeaveCriticalSection(&This->cs);
+    return S_OK;
+}
+
+static const INeutronWicGpuBitmap2Vtbl neutron_wicgpu2_vtbl =
+{
+    neutron_wicgpu2_QueryInterface,
+    neutron_wicgpu2_AddRef,
+    neutron_wicgpu2_Release,
+    neutron_wicgpu2_IsPristine,
+};
+
 static const IWICBitmapVtbl BitmapImpl_Vtbl = {
     BitmapImpl_QueryInterface,
     BitmapImpl_AddRef,
@@ -1023,6 +1085,10 @@
     This->gpu_ops = NULL;
     This->gpu_ctx = NULL;
     This->gpu_was_read = FALSE;
+    /* A bitmap built over caller memory (CreateBitmapFromMemory and friends) already holds
+     * pixels; one we calloc'd is transparent black and has nothing worth retaining. */
+    This->pristine = (view == NULL);
+    This->INeutronWicGpuBitmap2_iface.lpVtbl = &neutron_wicgpu2_vtbl;
     InitializeCriticalSectionEx(&This->cs, 0, RTL_CRITICAL_SECTION_FLAG_FORCE_DEBUG_INFO);
     This->cs.DebugInfo->Spare[0] = (DWORD_PTR)(__FILE__ ": BitmapImpl.lock");
 
--- a/include/wine/neutron_wicgpu.h
+++ b/include/wine/neutron_wicgpu.h
@@ -97,6 +97,43 @@
     const INeutronWicGpuBitmapVtbl *lpVtbl;
 };
 
+/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * v2: "has this bitmap ever held pixels?"
+ *
+ * A D2D render target over a WIC bitmap is RETAINED: the bitmap is the backing store, so a
+ * target created over a bitmap that already holds pixels must start out showing them (that
+ * is the time-ruler fix). Seeding costs a full-surface upload, and MEASURED in Premiere it
+ * is wasted almost every time -- 17k seeds in a minute of timeline scrolling, of which only
+ * 274 bitmaps had any content at all. The rest are freshly created and still all zeroes,
+ * where the correct and free answer is the plain clear the code did before.
+ *
+ * Deliberately a SEPARATE IID rather than a method appended to the v1 vtbl: d2d1 and
+ * windowscodecs are separate DLLs and a runtime can be assembled with one of them older
+ * (this project stages individual DLLs into runtimes routinely). A missing v2 simply fails
+ * QueryInterface and the caller seeds unconditionally, which is the old, correct, slower
+ * behaviour -- whereas a vtbl grown in place would be a call into whatever follows it.
+ * {6e657574-726f-6e02-77-69-63-67-70-75-30-32} -- "neutron" + "wicgpu02". */
+DEFINE_GUID(IID_INeutronWicGpuBitmap2, 0x6e657574,0x726f,0x6e02,0x77,0x69,0x63,0x67,0x70,0x75,0x30,0x32);
+
+typedef struct INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 INeutronWicGpuBitmap2;
+
+typedef struct INeutronWicGpuBitmap2Vtbl
+{
+    HRESULT (STDMETHODCALLTYPE *QueryInterface)(INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *iface, REFIID iid, void **out);
+    ULONG   (STDMETHODCALLTYPE *AddRef)(INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *iface);
+    ULONG   (STDMETHODCALLTYPE *Release)(INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *iface);
+
+    /* TRUE when no pixel of this bitmap has ever been written -- not by an initialising
+     * copy, not through a write lock, not by an attached GPU source. A pristine bitmap
+     * reads as fully transparent, so there is nothing for a render target to retain. */
+    HRESULT (STDMETHODCALLTYPE *IsPristine)(INeutronWicGpuBitmap2 *iface, BOOL *pristine);
+} INeutronWicGpuBitmap2Vtbl;
+
+struct INeutronWicGpuBitmap2
+{
+    const INeutronWicGpuBitmap2Vtbl *lpVtbl;
+};
+
 #define INeutronWicGpuBitmap_QueryInterface(p,a,b) (p)->lpVtbl->QueryInterface(p,a,b)
 #define INeutronWicGpuBitmap_AddRef(p)             (p)->lpVtbl->AddRef(p)
 #define INeutronWicGpuBitmap_Release(p)            (p)->lpVtbl->Release(p)
